{
  "name": "Public bucket leaks a key that unlocks an escalation-capable user",
  "phase7_note": "Phase 7: originally used iam:CreateAccessKey as the admin-equivalent permission. v2 treats that as a takeover of a specific user (CAN_TAKE_OVER), admin only if that user is, so this now uses the self-sufficient iam:AttachUserPolicy. The route shape under test is unchanged.",
  "ground_truth": "synthetic — the handbook's headline example (a public bucket of cat photos vs a public bucket holding credentials)",
  "story": "Two public-ish buckets. One is blocked by Public Access Block and holds nothing. The other is world-readable and contains a .env with a long-term access key belonging to deploy-bot, whose policy can call iam:AttachUserPolicy. This is the EXTERNAL source class: the path starts with no credential at all.",
